// Type checking for the gate self-tests (`scripts/**/*.test.ts`).
//
// These 16 test files were outside every tsconfig, so type errors in the tests
// themselves were invisible: a renamed field on a gate result, a misspelled
// option key, a wrong argument count or a nullable value used unguarded only
// surfaced at run time (or never, for branches a green run does not reach).
// This config closes that hole.
//
// Deliberate relaxations, and why they do not gut the check:
//
// * `allowJs` + `checkJs: false` - the gates themselves are untyped `.mjs`
//   (`scripts/*.mjs`, `scripts/contract/**`). Turning on `checkJs` would mean
//   retro-typing the whole gate implementation (thousands of lines), which is
//   out of scope for this task. `allowJs` still lets the compiler *read* those
//   modules so the tests get real shapes for what they import; checkJs:false
//   just keeps the compiler from reporting on the implementation itself.
//
// * `noImplicitAny: false` - destructuring / calling anything from those
//   untyped `.mjs` modules yields implicit `any`, so keeping the flag on would
//   bury the real errors under ~290 "implicitly has an 'any' type" reports
//   (measured: TS7005 x151, TS7006 x125, TS7034 x11).
//
// What is still enforced (inherited from ../tsconfig.base.json): `strict`
// (incl. `strictNullChecks`), `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`,
// `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, `isolatedModules`, `verbatimModuleSyntax`.
// So `tsc -p scripts` still fails on: misspelled / renamed fields on typed
// objects (TS2339/TS2353/TS2551), wrong argument count or type at a call site
// (TS2554/TS2345/TS2769), possible-undefined access (TS18048/TS2532), a
// nullable value passed where non-null is required (TS2345), incompatible
// assignments (TS2322), missing or unresolved imports (TS2304/TS2305/TS2307),
// and `unknown` used as if it were typed (TS18046). It will *not* catch a wrong
// member name on a value that came out of an untyped `.mjs` export, because
// that value is `any`; that is what the typed helpers in `scripts/lib/`
// (`test-assert.ts`) and the per-file shape types are for.
//
// `types: ["node"]` is enough: every test imports `vitest` explicitly, so no
// `vitest/globals` entry is needed. No `composite` / project references
// (route doc 05, R-08).
{
  "extends": "../tsconfig.base.json",
  "include": ["./**/*.ts"],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": false,
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "types": ["node"]
  }
}
